CHMOD(1)                       User Commands                        CHMOD(1)

NAME
    chmod - confirm a file's permission bits

SYNOPSIS
    chmod MODE PATH

DESCRIPTION
    Ask whether MODE, which is octal, one to four digits (for example 600
    or 0700), is true of PATH. There is no symbolic form, and a mode that
    does not parse is refused rather than partly applied.

    Who may reach a file is the filesystem policy's to say, not mode
    bits', so chmod stores nothing. It succeeds when it asks for what is
    already true: no set-id or sticky bit, group bits zero, other bits
    exactly what stat(1) reports for everyone else, and owner bits among
    your own rights. Otherwise it is refused. So chmod 600 on a file
    another account can also reach fails, and that is how you learn the
    file is shared. To change who may reach a file, change the policy or
    where the file lives.

SECURITY
    The fs_server decides, through the capability your session reached
    the file by, never against who you are or anything the shell says.

SEE ALSO
    stat(1), chown(8)
